import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const triangleCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'aperture', label: 'aperture (f/ number)', min: 1.4, max: 22, step: 0.2, value: 2.8, unit: '' },
    { name: 'shutter', label: 'shutter speed', min: 1, max: 15, step: 1, value: 6, unit: ' (2^)' },
    { name: 'iso', label: 'ISO', min: 100, max: 6400, step: 100, value: 200, unit: '' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'exposure value (EV)', expr: 'log2(aperture*aperture/(pow(2,shutter)/50))/log2(2)*(200/iso)', unit: ' (higher = darker)' },
    { label: 'depth of field trend', expr: 'aperture', unit: ' (higher = deeper)' },
  ],
};

const exposureCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Three dials, one bucket', note: 'Exposure is water in a bucket: aperture is the tap\'s width, shutter time is how long it runs, ISO is how sensitive the taste test is.' },
    { math: '2 · Aperture: f-numbers run backwards', note: 'f/2 is twice the diameter of f/4 and four times the light. Each full stop (f/2 → f/2.8 → f/4) halves the light.' },
    { math: '3 · Shutter: time is motion', note: 'Halving the time halves the light and freezes twice the speed. 1/1000 s freezes birds; 1/2 s turns waterfalls to silk.' },
    { math: '4 · ISO: sensitivity with a price', note: 'Raising ISO brightens digitally but multiplies noise. ISO is the escape dial when the other two cannot move.' },
    { math: '5 · Stops are the currency', note: 'One stop = a doubling or halving of light. The triangle is bookkeeping in stops: give up one dial, pay on another.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/photography/foundational/the-exposure-triangle">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Photography's technical core is one controlled equation with three dials: <strong>aperture</strong>
    (how wide the light tap is opened), <strong>shutter speed</strong> (how long it stays open)
    and <strong>ISO</strong> (how sensitive the sensor is to what arrives). The same "correct"
    exposure — the same amount of light recorded — can be made a thousand different ways, and
    which way you choose is not technical bookkeeping but the photograph's central creative
    decision, because each dial carries a side effect that changes the picture's meaning:
    depth, motion and grain.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic teaching analogy is a bucket under a tap. The <em>aperture</em> is the tap's
    width — a wide tap fills the bucket quickly; a narrow one trickles. The <em>shutter
    speed</em> is how long the tap runs. The bucket either fills exactly to the line (correct
    exposure), overflows (overexposed — highlights blown to pure white) or falls short
    (underexposed — shadows crushed to black). The <em>ISO</em> is stranger but essential: it
    is how sensitive your "taste test" is — the same half-full bucket is judged "correct" if
    you care less about completeness. Fill the bucket by many combinations of tap width and
    time and sensitivity: f/4 for 1/125 s at ISO 200, f/2.8 for 1/250 s at ISO 200, f/4 for
    1/250 s at ISO 400 — all the same brightness, all different photographs. The dials'
    side effects are the subject of the next section; first, the units.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="three dials, one bucket, and stops" widget="equationStep" config={exposureCfg} />

  <h2>The three dials: units, stops and the exchange rate</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Aperture</strong> is measured in f-numbers — the lens's focal length divided by
    the opening's diameter — and the scale runs backwards: <em>smaller</em> numbers mean
    <em>wider</em> openings. The reason is geometry: light-gathering scales with the opening's
    <em>area</em>, so f/2 (a 25 mm opening on a 50 mm lens) admits not twice but <em>four
    times</em> the light of f/4 (a 12.5 mm opening) — two "stops" of light. Each full stop on
    the standard scale (f/1.4, f/2, f/2.8, f/4, f/5.6, f/8, f/11, f/16, f/22) multiplies the
    opening's area by two, halving the light each step up. <strong>Shutter speed</strong> is
    simple time — 1 s, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/15, 1/30, 1/60, 1/125, 1/250, 1/500, 1/1000 s — and
    each step to the right halves the light and halves the motion the frame can capture.
    <strong>ISO</strong> scales how brightly the sensor's signal is rendered — 100, 200, 400,
    800, 1600, 3200 — each doubling of ISO is one stop of "free" brightness, bought at the
    price of amplified noise (grain in the shadows) and reduced highlight headroom. The
    universal currency across all three dials is the <strong>stop</strong>: one stop is a
    doubling or halving of light. The triangle's whole arithmetic is exchange in stops:
    open the aperture one stop and you may halve the shutter time, or drop the ISO one stop,
    and the bucket fills exactly as before. The slider bank below computes the exchange rate
    live — notice that large apertures are the small f-numbers, and that the depth-of-field
    readout tracks the f-number directly.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example grounds the bookkeeping in a scene every photographer meets. The
    "Sunny 16" rule says: in bright daylight, f/16 with shutter speed equal to 1/ISO —
    f/16 at 1/125 s with ISO 100 — gives correct exposure without a meter. But a portrait
    wants the background melted, so open to f/2.8: that is four stops wider (f/16 → f/11 →
    f/8 → f/5.6 → f/4 → f/2.8), so pay it back on the shutter — four stops faster:
    1/125 → 1/2000 s. Same bucket level, entirely different photograph: the portrait has
    razor-thin focus on the eyes and a dissolved background, while Sunny 16 had everything
    from nose to horizon sharp. Neither is "more correct"; the exchange just cashed the
    same light for different side effects.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the exchange rate between the dials" widget="sliderLab" config={triangleCfg} />

  <h2>Depth of field and motion: the side effects you choose on purpose</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Depth of field</strong> (DOF) is the range of distances that appear acceptably
    sharp, and it obeys four levers. Aperture: wider openings (smaller f-numbers) produce
    shallower focus — the light cones from out-of-focus points are wider and blur faster.
    Focal length: longer lenses shallow the focus at the same aperture and framing. Subject
    distance: the closer you focus, the thinner the plane of sharpness falls. Sensor size
    acts through the others: larger sensors invite longer focal lengths for the same
    framing, and thus shallower DOF — the physical reason full-frame portraits melt
    backgrounds more readily than phone cameras do. The genres fork here on purpose:
    portraits at f/1.8 isolate a face from its world; landscapes at f/11 (often focused at
    the <strong>hyperfocal distance</strong>, which maximises the sharp range from foreground
    to infinity) keep everything crisp; street photographers pick f/8 and let the deep focus
    forgive imperfect timing. The out-of-focus quality itself — <em>bokeh</em> — has become a
    lens-design obsession, because "how it melts" is part of the image's character.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Motion</strong> is the shutter's side effect, and it cuts both ways: blur as
    failure and blur as meaning. The freezing scale is roughly: 1/250 s handles walking
    people; 1/500–1/1000 s handles running and most sport; 1/2000 s and beyond freezes
    birds, water splashes and ball strikes. Handheld work adds the old reciprocal rule —
    keep the shutter no slower than 1/focal-length seconds (1/60 s on a 50 mm lens) unless
    stabilisation helps. But deliberate blur is a vocabulary: <strong>panning</strong>
    (following a cyclist at 1/30 s keeps the subject sharp while the world streaks — speed
    made visible), long exposures turn waterfalls to silk and traffic into light rivers
    (1/2 to 30 s, tripod mandatory, and in daylight a <strong>neutral-density filter</strong>
    — sunglasses for the lens — buys the missing stops), and star photography runs 10–25
    seconds before the Earth's rotation smears the points. The genres fork here too:
    the sports shooter pays everything for shutter speed (wide aperture, high ISO), the
    landscape shooter pays everything for depth and stillness (small aperture, ISO 100,
    tripod). Master photographers are not better at "correct" exposure — they are deliberate
    about which side effect to keep, and they know which dial to sacrifice first.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that one of the three dials is "the right way" to expose, or that high ISO is a failure. In the triangle's logic there is no correct dial — only correct <em>side effects for this picture</em>. The concert photographer raising ISO to 6400 is not compensating for ignorance; grain that preserves a moment beats noise-free blur of nothing, which is why ISO is honoured as the escape dial rather than shamed.</p>

  <h2>Metering and light: the camera's opinion, overridden</h2>
  <p>
    Exposure needs a measurement, and the camera's built-in meter is a <em>reflected-light</em>
    meter: it reads the light bouncing off the scene and assumes the scene, on average,
    reflects middle grey (the standard is roughly 18 per cent reflectance). The assumption is
    brilliant for average scenes and systematically wrong at the extremes — which is why snow
    photographs come back grey (the meter underexposes the brilliant white toward grey: add
    about +2 stops of compensation) and why a night scene or a black cat washes out (subtract
    compensation). Camera metering modes operationalise different opinions:
    <strong>evaluative/matrix</strong> metering reads the whole frame against a database of
    scenes; <strong>centre-weighted</strong> privileges the middle; <strong>spot</strong>
    meters a tiny circle — the tool for deliberately placing one tone (meter the highlight,
    open up; meter the shadow, stop down). The photographer's working instrument is
    <strong>exposure compensation</strong> (+/− in stops), nudging every automatic exposure
    off the meter's grey assumption — and the feedback loop is the <strong>histogram</strong>,
    the brightness distribution graphed left (black) to right (white): a histogram bunched
    against the right edge means clipped highlights (data gone forever), against the left
    means crushed shadows. Advanced practice "exposes to the right" — pushing the histogram
    as far toward the highlight edge as data survives, because highlights are harder to
    recover than shadows — and bracketing (shooting at −1, 0, +1) hedges high-contrast
    scenes, feeding the HDR merge.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scene's <strong>dynamic range</strong> — the span between its brightest meaningful
    highlight and deepest meaningful shadow — is the quiet variable that makes metering hard:
    a backlit portrait or a sunset interior can span 12–14 stops while the sensor captures
    maybe 10–14, so something must be sacrificed or blended. Ansel Adams built the
    <strong>Zone System</strong> on exactly this problem — placing shadows where the film
    would hold them and developing (in film chemistry) to control the highlights — and
    digital exposure inherits its logic: decide what must hold detail, meter for it, let the
    rest fall where it falls. In practice the triangle sets the physics, the meter guesses
    the scene, the histogram reports the truth, and the artist decides who is right — the
    daily loop of every deliberate photograph.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One more worked metering passage shows the grey-assumption logic under pressure, because
    spot metering is where the photographer finally outruns the camera. A stage scene: a
    singer lit by a single spotlight against a black theatre. Matrix metering averages the
    vast black with the lit face and overexposes badly — the face blows, the black turns
    grey murk. The disciplined alternative: switch to spot metering, take a reading from the
    singer's lit cheek (a Caucasian skin tone sits roughly one stop brighter than middle
    grey, so the meter's "make it grey" instruction means the photographer opens about one
    stop to restore the skin to skin), lock that exposure, and let the background fall to
    true black — which is not underexposure but the photograph's intent. Dark skin reverses
    the correction; snow and bridal dresses repeat the first lesson at +2; a grey card held
    in the subject's light eliminates the guesswork entirely, because the card <em>is</em>
    the middle grey the meter seeks. Every one of these moves is the same sentence:
    the meter reports how to make the measured surface grey, and the photographer decides
    which surfaces deserve to stay what they are.
  </p>
  <p>
    Four refinements carry the triangle into advanced work. <strong>The EV system</strong>:
    exposure value condenses the dials into one number — EV = log<sub>2</sub>(N²/t), with N
    the f-number and t the shutter time in seconds — and the slider bank's EV readout is this
    arithmetic at work; light meters, flash guides and old cameras all speak it, and "EV 15"
    is the formal name of Sunny 16. <strong>Reciprocity and its failure</strong>: the
    equivalence principle (a stop here buys a stop there) holds tightly for digital sensors
    across their working range, but film breaks down at very long exposures —
    <em>reciprocity failure</em> demands extra exposure past a second or so — and digital has
    its own edge effects: long-exposure heat noise, and electronic shutters introducing
    rolling-shutter distortion at fast pan speeds. <strong>Sensor formats and equivalence</strong>:
    the same f-number admits the same light <em>per area</em>, but a larger sensor's total
    area gathers more total light at the same exposure — which is why full-frame images are
    cleaner at the same ISO, why "equivalent aperture" arguments about DOF and noise rage
    online, and why the triangle's dials must be quoted with the sensor they belong to for
    strict comparisons. <strong>Flash changes the game</strong>: a flash pulse freezes motion
    itself (duration around 1/1000 s), the shutter no longer controls ambient-and-flash
    separately (the sync-speed limit appears), and the "triangle" becomes a tetrahedron of
    ambient exposure plus flash power — the reason studio photographers think in f-stops and
    power ratios, not shutter speeds. The constants through all of it are the ones this page
    taught: stops are the currency, every dial's side effect is a creative choice, the meter
    is an opinion about grey, and the histogram is the only witness that does not flatter
    anyone.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> AQA A-level Art &amp; Design (Photography) specification; Freeman, <em>The Photographer's Exposure Field Guide</em>; Ansel Adams, <em>The Negative</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
